;; The sandbox the broker runs in.
;;
;; The broker (mitmdump with proxy.py) is the one program on the Mac that
;; reads what the agent VM sends. It is a large program, and a bug in it
;; could let a hostile program in the VM run code on the Mac. Without this
;; file that code would have all of the user's rights: every file, every
;; key, every program. With it, macOS itself holds the broker to the short
;; list below, and nothing the broker does can lift that.
;;
;; This is a profile for macOS's built-in sandbox, put on by
;; /usr/bin/sandbox-exec just before mitmdump starts; see `brokerRun` in
;; ../darwin/commands.nix. The language is a small Scheme. A rule names an
;; operation (reading a file, connecting out) and what it applies to.
;;
;; The start command passes three values:
;;
;;   BROKER_DIR  the broker's own folder, `broker` in the state folder,
;;               as a full path without symbolic links
;;   PORT        the port the broker listens on
;;   RULES       the rules file, as a full path without symbolic links
;;
;; This file is not the whole profile. When the commands are built, rules
;; for the Nix store are added below it: reading the packages mitmproxy is
;; made of and the broker's own scripts, and starting mitmdump. They are
;; worked out from the packages, so they cannot be written here by hand;
;; `brokerSandbox` in ../darwin/commands.nix adds them, and
;; tests/broker-sandbox.sh shows the result.
;;
;; What the sandbox cannot hold back is in docs/threat-model.md, under
;; "The broker is confined".
;;
;; To change this file: run tests/broker-sandbox.sh, which tries each thing
;; below from inside the sandbox, and tests/github-broker.sh, which runs a
;; whole broker in it. A rule that is missing shows as a broker that does
;; not start; macOS writes what it refused to its log:
;;
;;   log show --last 2m --predicate 'sender == "Sandbox"' | grep deny

(version 1)

;; Everything is refused unless a rule below allows it. That covers files,
;; the network, starting programs, and talking to other programs and to the
;; system's services. So the list below is all the broker can do. In
;; particular there is no rule for:
;;
;;   process-fork  the broker cannot start a second process, so it can run
;;                 no program beside itself
;;   mach-lookup   it cannot talk to the system's services: the keychain,
;;                 the clipboard, the screen, other programs
;;   reading or writing anywhere in the home folder but its own folder
(deny default)

;; --- Reading -----------------------------------------------------------

;; The parts of macOS that every program is made of: the system's
;; libraries. Nothing of the user's is in these folders.
(allow file-read*
  (subpath "/usr/lib")
  (subpath "/System/Library")
  (subpath "/System/Volumes/Preboot/Cryptexes/OS")
  (subpath "/System/Cryptexes/OS"))

;; The time zone, so that the times in the log are the Mac's.
(allow file-read*
  (literal "/private/etc/localtime")
  (subpath "/private/var/db/timezone")
  (subpath "/usr/share/zoneinfo")
  (subpath "/usr/share/zoneinfo.default"))

;; Random numbers for the encryption, and the place where unwanted output
;; goes.
(allow file-read*
  (literal "/dev/null")
  (literal "/dev/random")
  (literal "/dev/urandom"))
(allow file-write-data (literal "/dev/null"))

;; A program looks at each folder on the way to a file. This allows seeing
;; that the folders above the allowed ones exist, and what kind of thing
;; they are. It does not allow listing them or reading anything in them.
(allow file-read-metadata
  (literal "/")
  (literal "/nix")
  (literal "/nix/store")
  (literal "/etc")
  (literal "/var")
  (literal "/tmp")
  (literal "/private")
  (literal "/private/etc")
  (literal "/private/var")
  (literal "/private/var/db")
  (literal "/usr")
  (literal "/usr/share")
  (literal "/System")
  (literal "/dev")
  (path-ancestors (param "BROKER_DIR"))
  (path-ancestors (param "RULES")))

;; The rules. By default they are the repo's copy in the Nix store. A file
;; named in AGENT_BROKER_RULES for `nix run .#broker` can be anywhere, so
;; this one file is allowed by its name.
(allow file-read* (literal (param "RULES")))

;; --- Reading and writing ------------------------------------------------

;; The broker's own folder, where mitmproxy keeps its certificate authority.
;; This is the only place the broker can create, change or delete a file.
;;
;; The log is not in it and needs no rule: the start command opens the log
;; and hands it to the broker already open, as its output. The same goes
;; for the pipe the tokens arrive on. The sandbox is asked when a file is
;; opened, not when an open one is written to.
(allow file-read* file-write* (subpath (param "BROKER_DIR")))

;; --- The network ---------------------------------------------------------

;; Listen where the VM's connections arrive: this Mac's own address, the
;; broker's port. The profile language knows two names for an address,
;; "localhost" and "*" (any), so "localhost" stands for 127.0.0.1 here.
(allow network-bind network-inbound
  (local ip (string-append "localhost:" (param "PORT"))))

;; Connect out to port 443, the port of HTTPS, on any machine. The profile
;; language cannot tell machines apart by name or by address, so which
;; hosts the broker connects to stays the job of proxy.py and rules.yaml.
;; What this rule adds is that other ports are out of reach.
(allow network-outbound (remote ip "*:443"))

;; But not to programs on this Mac. Many of them trust whatever comes from
;; the Mac itself. A later rule wins over an earlier one, so this takes
;; port 443 of the Mac's own address back out of the rule above.
(deny network-outbound (remote ip "localhost:*"))

;; Looking up a host's address. macOS does that in a service of its own,
;; which a program reaches through this socket.
(allow network-outbound (literal "/private/var/run/mDNSResponder"))

;; --- What a program needs to run at all ---------------------------------

;; The top folder itself. macOS's loader opens it when a program starts,
;; and no program starts without this. It holds only the names of the
;; system's main folders.
(allow file-read-data (literal "/"))

;; Reading facts about the machine, such as the number of processors.
(allow sysctl-read)
